1
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:20,714
<i>Susan, you've got to hit
the board to pop the balloon.</i>

2
00:00:35,035 --> 00:00:37,197
<i>Susan, throw again.</i>

3
00:00:44,344 --> 00:00:46,609
<i>Pick another card,
don't be upset.</i>

4
00:00:56,356 --> 00:00:58,052
<i>Throw again.</i>

5
00:01:04,398 --> 00:01:06,663
<i>Freezer. Susan, you did it.</i>

6
00:04:51,458 --> 00:04:52,983
- Where is he?
- Oh, he'll be here.

7
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,629
He probably just, uh,
got caught in traffic.

8
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:57,196
I bet he ran out on me.

9
00:04:57,364 --> 00:05:00,425
Probably figured the whole thing was
hopeless, he took off for the Bahamas.

10
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,092
My father would not do that.

11
00:05:03,870 --> 00:05:06,101
He hates the Bahamas.

12
00:05:06,606 --> 00:05:11,977
- Oh, you can't smoke in here.
- I tell you what, I'm not gonna light it.

13
00:05:12,145 --> 00:05:15,638
I'm just gonna sit it in my
mouth and pretend. See?

14
00:05:15,816 --> 00:05:17,751
Why don't you just quit?

15
00:05:18,285 --> 00:05:20,550
I give up drinking, I end
up on trial for murder.

16
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:22,086
Forget it.

17
00:05:22,255 --> 00:05:24,850
Besides, everybody's
gotta have at least one vice.

18
00:05:25,025 --> 00:05:28,518
Otherwise the angels get jealous
and start screwing up your life.

19
00:05:28,695 --> 00:05:31,688
That's true. A nun told me that.

20
00:05:33,767 --> 00:05:35,929
- Where've you been?
- I'm sorry I'm late.

21
00:05:36,102 --> 00:05:37,900
What the hell happened?

22
00:05:38,071 --> 00:05:40,905
- I fell asleep on the couch.
- You did what?

23
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:42,906
Are you okay?

24
00:05:57,123 --> 00:06:00,719
That's Paul Dodge, the man
who really killed Allan Rafferty.

25
00:06:00,894 --> 00:06:02,954
Dad, we've been through this.
- No, no, no.

26
00:06:03,129 --> 00:06:05,792
I know how it happened. I
just had a dream about it.

27
00:06:06,132 --> 00:06:08,465
See, he wanted to frame Terry,

28
00:06:08,635 --> 00:06:11,696
so he opened the door
at the end of the east wing,

29
00:06:11,872 --> 00:06:14,637
knowing it would set off the
alarm at the security guard's desk.

30
00:06:14,808 --> 00:06:17,403
Then he backed into the shadows.

31
00:06:17,577 --> 00:06:21,014
Security guard walked down
the east wing to check the door.

32
00:06:21,181 --> 00:06:22,809
Paul Dodge waited
for him to pass,

33
00:06:22,983 --> 00:06:26,476
then he hurried back up
the west wing into the pool,

34
00:06:26,653 --> 00:06:31,421
drowned Allan Rafferty, threw
Terry's necklace in the pool,

35
00:06:31,591 --> 00:06:35,426
and then snuck back to his suite,
which was just across the hall.

36
00:06:35,595 --> 00:06:36,654
It works for me.

37
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:40,892
But Dr. Dodge's rooms are in the
west. He'd have to go right past the guard

38
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:43,195
to get to the door
in the east wing.

39
00:06:43,370 --> 00:06:45,737
Ben, how'd he do that?

40
00:06:46,006 --> 00:06:47,030
I don't know.

41
00:06:49,075 --> 00:06:50,566
Dad,

42
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:53,337
you're obsessed with the idea

43
00:06:53,513 --> 00:06:56,608
that Paul Dodge did it, and
your theory doesn't work.

44
00:06:56,783 --> 00:07:00,185
You're not looking at
the facts objectively.

45
00:07:00,353 --> 00:07:03,323
How come you're so pale?

46
00:07:04,124 --> 00:07:07,925
You're the one that keeps
telling me to stay in out of the sun.

47
00:07:09,896 --> 00:07:12,161
Come on, let's get inside.

48
00:07:16,269 --> 00:07:18,029
Is this the necklace
you fished out

49
00:07:18,171 --> 00:07:20,834
at the clinic that night, detective?
- Yes, it is.

50
00:07:21,007 --> 00:07:22,847
Were you able to ascertain
to whom it belonged?

51
00:07:23,009 --> 00:07:26,946
Yes, sir. It's engraved with the words,
uh, "To Terry with love, from Linda."

52
00:07:27,113 --> 00:07:29,139
And since Terry Landis
was the only Terry

53
00:07:29,316 --> 00:07:31,444
staying at the, uh,
clinic at that time,

54
00:07:31,618 --> 00:07:34,452
we went up to his room, asked
him if it was his, he said it was.

55
00:07:35,121 --> 00:07:38,121
Did he explain to you how it was
found in the pool with Dr. Rafferty's body?

56
00:07:38,258 --> 00:07:39,978
No, he said he didn't
know how it got there.

57
00:07:40,126 --> 00:07:42,887
Did he suggest he might have lost it
while swimming laps or something?

58
00:07:43,029 --> 00:07:46,227
No, he told us he never used the
pool, said the chlorine dried his hair out.

59
00:07:46,866 --> 00:07:48,528
You've worked homicide how long?

60
00:07:48,702 --> 00:07:49,726
Eight years.

61
00:07:49,903 --> 00:07:52,903
In your opinion, how do you
suggest this necklace got in the pool?

62
00:07:53,039 --> 00:07:54,959
You think it might have
just fallen off his neck.

63
00:07:55,108 --> 00:07:56,628
Or maybe it was
yanked off accidentally

64
00:07:56,776 --> 00:07:59,405
while he was
drowning Dr. Rafferty.

65
00:07:59,579 --> 00:08:00,911
Could have.

66
00:08:01,548 --> 00:08:04,416
Thank you, that's all.
- Mr. Matlock.

67
00:08:05,852 --> 00:08:07,514
Detective,

68
00:08:09,689 --> 00:08:11,351
does this, uh,

69
00:08:12,392 --> 00:08:14,190
clasp look broken to you?

70
00:08:14,361 --> 00:08:15,385
No.

71
00:08:15,562 --> 00:08:18,794
But if it had been yanked
off in some violent struggle,

72
00:08:18,965 --> 00:08:20,763
you'd think it would be
broken, wouldn't you?

73
00:08:20,934 --> 00:08:23,426
Well, like he said,
maybe it just fell off.

74
00:08:23,603 --> 00:08:26,163
- Off the defendant's neck?
- Yes.

75
00:08:27,507 --> 00:08:29,135
Or maybe,

76
00:08:29,309 --> 00:08:33,371
maybe somebody stole
this out of Terry's room,

77
00:08:33,546 --> 00:08:37,005
and dropped it in the pool in
order to frame him, right, detective?

78
00:08:38,651 --> 00:08:40,711
- I guess so.
- Thank you.

79
00:08:44,958 --> 00:08:47,158
I understand you were a
patient at the Paul Dodge Clinic

80
00:08:47,327 --> 00:08:49,023
at the time Dr. Rafferty
was murdered.

81
00:08:49,195 --> 00:08:51,061
I'm still a patient there.

82
00:08:51,231 --> 00:08:53,992
Now, unlike many celebrities who've
admitted themselves to the clinic,

83
00:08:54,134 --> 00:08:56,262
you seem to have made
no attempt to keep it a secret.

84
00:08:56,436 --> 00:08:57,961
Why is that, Miss Page?

85
00:08:58,138 --> 00:09:02,007
I believe people like me set an
example, whether we like it or not.

86
00:09:02,175 --> 00:09:05,771
Whatever is cool for us is gonna be
cool for the kids who dig our music.

87
00:09:05,945 --> 00:09:08,176
We do drugs, they're
gonna do drugs.

88
00:09:08,348 --> 00:09:11,284
We discover that that was a
mistake and go and get help,

89
00:09:11,451 --> 00:09:12,749
maybe they will too.

90
00:09:13,853 --> 00:09:15,412
The defendant, Terry Landis,

91
00:09:15,588 --> 00:09:17,853
did he let people know he'd
been admitted to the clinic?

92
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:21,256
- He didn't want anybody to know.
- He ever tell you why?

93
00:09:21,428 --> 00:09:23,397
He was afraid that it
was bad for his image.

94
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:25,532
He plays this cop on TV.

95
00:09:25,698 --> 00:09:28,429
He was afraid the network would can
him if it became common knowledge

96
00:09:28,601 --> 00:09:30,832
that he was a major
boozer in real life.

97
00:09:31,004 --> 00:09:32,996
I understand that on
the day of the murder

98
00:09:33,173 --> 00:09:35,093
Terry Landis came bursting
into a private session

99
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:37,161
you were having with
Dr. Rafferty. Is that right?

100
00:09:37,310 --> 00:09:39,074
- Yeah.
- Tell us what happened.

101
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:40,975
He was angry because
the <i>National Informer</i>

102
00:09:41,147 --> 00:09:42,809
had just come out
with a story about him.

103
00:09:48,054 --> 00:09:50,023
This the story? PAGE: Yes.

104
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:52,421
He was holding a
copy of it in his hand.

105
00:09:52,592 --> 00:09:55,255
He accused Dr. Rafferty of
leaking things that he had told him

106
00:09:55,428 --> 00:09:58,296
during his therapy
session to the <i>Informer.</i>

107
00:09:58,465 --> 00:10:00,900
What did Dr. Rafferty say?
- He denied it.

108
00:10:01,067 --> 00:10:03,866
What did Mr. Landis say?
- He didn't believe him.

109
00:10:04,037 --> 00:10:06,836
- He threaten Dr. Rafferty?
- More or less.

110
00:10:07,006 --> 00:10:10,326
In point of fact, didn't he throw a punch
at Dr. Rafferty right there in the office?

111
00:10:10,477 --> 00:10:11,809
Yes, but he missed.

112
00:10:11,978 --> 00:10:14,538
He's not very good
with his fists in real life.

113
00:10:15,448 --> 00:10:17,849
What about the necklace?
Was he wearing it?

114
00:10:18,017 --> 00:10:20,680
I don't know. Seems he
wore it most of the time.

115
00:10:20,854 --> 00:10:22,914
Thank you, Miss
Page. That's all.

116
00:10:24,390 --> 00:10:25,414
Mr. Matlock.

117
00:10:26,659 --> 00:10:28,252
No questions, Your Honor.

118
00:10:28,428 --> 00:10:30,829
The witness may step down.

119
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:42,654
It's not going very well, is it?

120
00:10:42,809 --> 00:10:45,005
- Oh, it's going fine.
- Oh, I know it's not.

121
00:10:45,178 --> 00:10:47,204
For one thing, juror
number 10 hates me.

122
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:49,260
- Why do you say that?
- The way he looks at me.

123
00:10:49,415 --> 00:10:51,577
Only directors
look at me like that.

124
00:10:51,751 --> 00:10:55,153
- Directors hate me. MATLOCK:
Maybe the security guard

125
00:10:55,321 --> 00:10:57,654
always takes a break
at the same time.

126
00:10:57,857 --> 00:10:59,951
Maybe Paul Dodge knew that.

127
00:11:00,126 --> 00:11:02,425
Maybe that's when he
snuck into the east wing.

128
00:11:02,595 --> 00:11:03,961
- Dad.
- Maybe that's when he snuck

129
00:11:04,130 --> 00:11:05,826
into Terry's room
and stole his necklace.

130
00:11:05,999 --> 00:11:07,058
Dad, give it a rest.

131
00:11:07,233 --> 00:11:09,896
Yeah. He killed two
birds with one stone.

132
00:11:10,069 --> 00:11:12,061
He waited for the
guard to leave his desk.

133
00:11:12,238 --> 00:11:15,675
Then he snuck out of his office in
the west wing, snuck to the east wing,

134
00:11:15,842 --> 00:11:18,869
snuck into Terry's room, stole
his necklace while he was asleep,

135
00:11:19,045 --> 00:11:20,877
then did that
thing with the door,

136
00:11:21,047 --> 00:11:23,846
making it look like Terry
had snuck into the east wing,

137
00:11:24,017 --> 00:11:28,512
snuck into the west wing, snuck
into the pool to kill Allan Rafferty.

138
00:11:29,756 --> 00:11:32,692
- This is not like you.
- Why?

139
00:11:32,859 --> 00:11:34,623
You never say "snuck."

140
00:11:36,963 --> 00:11:38,727
Just trying to
give it a little color.

141
00:11:39,899 --> 00:11:42,528
I'm gonna call that
guard right now.

142
00:11:43,236 --> 00:11:45,467
Oh, this is not good.

143
00:11:47,073 --> 00:11:48,097
Did you see that?

144
00:11:48,274 --> 00:11:52,143
My dad walked right past a hot dog
vendor and didn't even look at him.

145
00:11:53,980 --> 00:11:56,882
Something is very wrong.

146
00:12:05,758 --> 00:12:07,488
Dad.

147
00:12:09,262 --> 00:12:11,424
In here.

148
00:12:13,433 --> 00:12:15,402
What have you been doing?

149
00:12:22,842 --> 00:12:24,538
I don't know.

150
00:12:24,711 --> 00:12:27,909
- You don't know?
- No.

151
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:31,683
I called the
guard, and then I...

152
00:12:31,851 --> 00:12:33,683
I guess I fell asleep.

153
00:12:34,053 --> 00:12:36,022
You took another nap?

154
00:12:37,090 --> 00:12:41,994
The guard said that he didn't leave his
desk that night until the alarm went off.

155
00:12:42,161 --> 00:12:48,294
Dr. Dodge couldn't have slipped
into Terry's room without being seen.

156
00:12:49,369 --> 00:12:51,201
Why are you rubbing
your chest like that?

157
00:12:52,839 --> 00:12:54,933
Heartburn or something.

158
00:12:55,108 --> 00:12:58,567
- Why are you sweating like that?
- Maybe a little bit of flu.

159
00:12:59,579 --> 00:13:00,877
You don't feel well?

160
00:13:01,047 --> 00:13:04,950
I'm tired, Leanne. This Dodge
thing's been keeping me up at night.

161
00:13:05,451 --> 00:13:08,182
Oh, nothing doing, Dad.

162
00:13:08,354 --> 00:13:12,553
- You lie right back down.
- Leanne, I'm fine.

163
00:13:12,992 --> 00:13:16,053
The hell you are.
I'm calling 911.

164
00:13:16,229 --> 00:13:19,256
- No.
- Dad... Damn it, Dad.

165
00:13:35,815 --> 00:13:37,784
- What'd they say?
- Nothing.

166
00:13:38,217 --> 00:13:39,344
- Nothing.
- No.

167
00:13:39,519 --> 00:13:41,215
You tell them you
passed out this morning?

168
00:13:41,387 --> 00:13:42,980
- No.
- You didn't tell them?

169
00:13:43,156 --> 00:13:44,249
I didn't pass out.

170
00:13:44,424 --> 00:13:47,690
What do you call falling asleep on
the couch at 10 in the morning, Dad?

171
00:13:47,860 --> 00:13:50,056
I took a nap, I didn't pass out.

172
00:13:50,229 --> 00:13:51,925
- Give me my clothes.
- Why?

173
00:13:52,098 --> 00:13:53,396
- I'm going home.
- No.

174
00:13:53,566 --> 00:13:55,694
No, no. You are
not going anywhere

175
00:13:55,868 --> 00:13:58,235
until we talk to the doctor
and find out what's wrong.

176
00:13:58,404 --> 00:14:01,033
There's nothing wrong
with me. It was heartburn.

177
00:14:01,207 --> 00:14:03,301
- It's gone now. Give me my clothes.
- No.

178
00:14:04,744 --> 00:14:06,337
Mr. Matlock.

179
00:14:06,512 --> 00:14:08,913
Dr. Schoenfeld.

180
00:14:09,349 --> 00:14:13,445
Well, the good news is whatever it
was responsible for your symptoms,

181
00:14:13,619 --> 00:14:16,555
it doesn't appear to have
been a myocardial infarction.

182
00:14:17,423 --> 00:14:19,915
- What's that?
- A heart attack.

183
00:14:20,093 --> 00:14:24,394
- Give me my clothes.
- What is the bad news, doctor?

184
00:14:24,564 --> 00:14:27,932
Well, the bad news is that we don't
know what caused your symptoms.

185
00:14:28,167 --> 00:14:31,365
We'd like to keep you
overnight, do some more tests.

186
00:14:31,537 --> 00:14:33,028
- That would be fine.
- Overnight?

187
00:14:33,206 --> 00:14:34,572
- Dad.
- Now, Mr. Matlock,

188
00:14:34,741 --> 00:14:38,542
it's just one night. You want to
find out what's wrong, don't you?

189
00:14:38,711 --> 00:14:40,304
It was heartburn.

190
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:43,939
- It wasn't heartburn.
- Thank you, doctor.

191
00:14:44,117 --> 00:14:46,518
Dad, you just rest right here.

192
00:14:46,686 --> 00:14:50,623
I want you to be very comfortable.
I'm gonna take care of everything.

193
00:14:55,161 --> 00:14:57,630
It was heartburn.

194
00:15:04,937 --> 00:15:06,963
What about Terry
Landis' allegation

195
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:11,236
that Dr. Rafferty was selling
confidential information to that tabloid?

196
00:15:11,411 --> 00:15:14,904
Allan Rafferty was a highly
respected psychotherapist

197
00:15:15,081 --> 00:15:18,415
with impeccable credentials. He
would never have done such a thing.

198
00:15:18,584 --> 00:15:20,780
- But if he had...
- He didn't.

199
00:15:20,953 --> 00:15:23,047
But if he had, what
would you have done?

200
00:15:23,222 --> 00:15:26,249
I would have fired
him on the spot.

201
00:15:26,426 --> 00:15:28,588
If you'd done that, Terry
could've turned right around

202
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:34,257
and sold everything he knew about
your famous patients to the <i>Informer.</i>

203
00:15:36,068 --> 00:15:38,731
- What are you getting at?
- Motive.

204
00:15:38,905 --> 00:15:42,933
I think you suspected Dr. Rafferty
had leaked that information.

205
00:15:43,109 --> 00:15:48,639
So rather than risk being embarrassed
or ruined by him in the future,

206
00:15:48,815 --> 00:15:51,979
you killed him, and then pinned
the murder on Terry Landis

207
00:15:52,151 --> 00:15:55,349
to discredit him and
divert suspicion from you.

208
00:16:00,026 --> 00:16:02,086
I think not.

209
00:16:13,239 --> 00:16:16,004
I'm right. I know
it and so do you.

210
00:16:16,509 --> 00:16:18,444
You're daft, Mrs. Mclntyre.

211
00:16:18,611 --> 00:16:21,513
Twenty years ago I
could've had you committed.

212
00:16:22,381 --> 00:16:24,213
Once I figure out how
you got past the guard

213
00:16:24,383 --> 00:16:27,581
to trip that alarm in the
east wing, I am home free.

214
00:16:27,753 --> 00:16:31,417
- Oh, no one told you.
- About what?

215
00:16:31,591 --> 00:16:35,494
About the envelope I
gave the guard that night.

216
00:16:38,431 --> 00:16:40,332
What envelope?

217
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,931
I came out of my
rooms that night,

218
00:16:44,103 --> 00:16:46,129
went down the hall
to the front desk,

219
00:16:46,305 --> 00:16:47,500
gave the guard an envelope

220
00:16:47,673 --> 00:16:50,233
that was to be picked
up at 7 the next morning.

221
00:16:50,409 --> 00:16:54,710
Then I walked back
down the hall to my rooms.

222
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:56,747
What time was that?

223
00:16:57,617 --> 00:16:59,950
The guard always makes
notes of such things.

224
00:17:00,119 --> 00:17:04,113
I gave him the package
at exactly 10:27.

225
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:09,319
One minute later, after he watched
me walk up the hall to the west wing,

226
00:17:09,495 --> 00:17:12,329
the door down the
hall, around the corner,

227
00:17:12,498 --> 00:17:16,230
at the end of the
east wing was opened.

228
00:17:16,736 --> 00:17:20,002
Now, these are my rooms.
Here at the end of the west wing.

229
00:17:20,172 --> 00:17:25,110
Do you really think I could've
walked past that guard,

230
00:17:25,278 --> 00:17:28,806
slipped past him, in
less than a minute?

231
00:17:29,882 --> 00:17:32,647
I didn't know
about the envelope.

232
00:17:33,286 --> 00:17:34,914
Your father knew.

233
00:17:35,555 --> 00:17:38,150
I guess he forgot to tell you.

234
00:17:39,191 --> 00:17:40,887
Dad?

235
00:17:42,662 --> 00:17:44,460
Dad?

236
00:17:45,398 --> 00:17:46,832
Dad?

237
00:17:48,301 --> 00:17:50,668
Dad, can you hear me?

238
00:17:52,204 --> 00:17:55,834
Dad. Dad.

239
00:17:56,008 --> 00:17:57,738
Dad.

240
00:17:58,144 --> 00:17:59,806
Dad.

241
00:18:01,914 --> 00:18:03,394
I didn't tell you
about the envelope.

242
00:18:03,549 --> 00:18:06,280
Dad, you have got to get
this case out of your head.

243
00:18:06,452 --> 00:18:10,014
Your pulse is very rapid. I think
you'd better listen to your daughter.

244
00:18:10,189 --> 00:18:11,384
Did you hear that?

245
00:18:11,557 --> 00:18:13,423
I'll look in on you in an hour.

246
00:18:15,728 --> 00:18:17,287
Leanne, I can't stay here.

247
00:18:17,463 --> 00:18:18,795
- I'm in the middle...
- We...

248
00:18:18,965 --> 00:18:21,628
We. We are in the
middle of a case.

249
00:18:21,801 --> 00:18:24,430
I can take care of it.
It's only for a day or two.

250
00:18:25,438 --> 00:18:28,101
I hate hospitals, Leanne.

251
00:18:28,908 --> 00:18:30,740
There's sick people everywhere.

252
00:18:33,813 --> 00:18:35,782
You're afraid they'll find
something, aren't you?

253
00:18:37,149 --> 00:18:39,983
They're not gonna find anything.

254
00:18:40,152 --> 00:18:42,246
I'm afraid too.

255
00:18:42,955 --> 00:18:46,289
I wish I was good at
saying stuff like this.

256
00:18:47,493 --> 00:18:50,930
You're not in this alone, okay?

257
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:03,603
Hi.

258
00:19:05,244 --> 00:19:08,305
- Hello, Cliff.
- Hi. How's Ben?

259
00:19:08,481 --> 00:19:11,451
They're doing some tests. We'll
know more in a couple of days.

260
00:19:11,617 --> 00:19:14,587
I hope he'll be okay. Here.

261
00:19:14,787 --> 00:19:16,547
Does he still think
Paul Dodge is the killer?

262
00:19:16,689 --> 00:19:19,158
Yeah, that's all he talks about.

263
00:19:19,725 --> 00:19:22,092
Anyway, we know someone
at that clinic is the killer.

264
00:19:22,261 --> 00:19:24,753
I have the feeling the only
way we're gonna find out who

265
00:19:24,930 --> 00:19:27,798
is to work from the inside.

266
00:19:27,967 --> 00:19:29,697
You mean send
someone in under cover

267
00:19:29,869 --> 00:19:35,308
- to pose as a patient or something.
- Yeah.

268
00:19:35,708 --> 00:19:37,233
Yes.

269
00:19:41,781 --> 00:19:43,181
No.

270
00:19:43,349 --> 00:19:44,681
No. No.

271
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:46,079
No. Leanne, I'm a lawyer,

272
00:19:46,252 --> 00:19:47,972
- I'm not a spy.
- Cliff, all you have to do

273
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:50,240
is pretend like you're
screwed up for a couple of days

274
00:19:50,389 --> 00:19:52,949
- and keep your eyes and ears open.
- I'm not good at pretending.

275
00:19:53,092 --> 00:19:54,890
You know, when I
do, my palms sweat

276
00:19:55,061 --> 00:19:59,897
and my face goes flush,
and I got chest pains, and...

277
00:20:00,066 --> 00:20:03,298
- They're gonna know I'm lying.
- They'll think you're sick.

278
00:20:08,274 --> 00:20:09,833
Okay.

279
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:17,814
Of course I told him everything.
Dr. Rafferty was my shrink.

280
00:20:17,983 --> 00:20:20,282
Oh. So he knew all your secrets.

281
00:20:20,519 --> 00:20:23,284
Maybe he didn't know them
all. But he was getting there.

282
00:20:23,456 --> 00:20:25,925
It's rumored your
past is pretty colorful.

283
00:20:26,092 --> 00:20:29,756
My past is a walking,
talking museum of horrors.

284
00:20:29,929 --> 00:20:34,025
Come on, lady. Life is short. What
is it that you really wanna know?

285
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:35,280
What went through your mind

286
00:20:35,434 --> 00:20:37,869
when Terry burst into
Dr. Rafferty's office that day

287
00:20:38,037 --> 00:20:41,303
and accused him of selling
those stories to the tabloids?

288
00:20:41,474 --> 00:20:42,965
Do you remember?

289
00:20:43,142 --> 00:20:45,008
No, I don't.

290
00:20:45,878 --> 00:20:47,718
Sure you didn't think
there might be some truth

291
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:49,246
into what Terry was saying?

292
00:20:49,415 --> 00:20:53,648
And if Dr. Rafferty sold Terry out
like that, he might just sell you out too?

293
00:20:53,819 --> 00:20:56,379
Are you trying to say
that I killed Dr. Rafferty?

294
00:20:57,056 --> 00:20:59,890
Your room is in the east
wing, just like Terry's was.

295
00:21:00,059 --> 00:21:01,925
Allan Rafferty was
no threat to me.

296
00:21:02,094 --> 00:21:04,393
Like I said on the
stand, I got no secrets.

297
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:07,089
It's one thing to announce to
the world that you're an addict,

298
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:10,430
but making public why you're an
addict's got to be a lot more painful.

299
00:21:10,603 --> 00:21:12,367
I'm not afraid of the truth.

300
00:21:12,538 --> 00:21:13,562
Nothing happened to me

301
00:21:13,739 --> 00:21:17,699
that doesn't happen to tens of
thousands of girls out there every day.

302
00:21:17,877 --> 00:21:21,473
Now, as far as I'm concerned,
making that public could only help.

303
00:21:22,782 --> 00:21:25,775
Now, I gotta get to
group. Tell Terry I said hey.

304
00:21:29,488 --> 00:21:31,821
Well, everything
looks fine, Mr. Lewis.

305
00:21:31,991 --> 00:21:35,393
Soon as Dr. Dodge talks to you, I'll
help you get settled into your room.

306
00:21:35,561 --> 00:21:38,656
Uh, Dr. Dodge? The
Dr. Dodge who runs the place?

307
00:21:38,864 --> 00:21:41,891
Don't worry. He interviews
all the new patients.

308
00:21:42,067 --> 00:21:43,763
It's standard
operating procedure.

309
00:21:52,845 --> 00:21:55,940
Now, he'll be right with
you. You just have a seat.

310
00:21:57,383 --> 00:21:58,851
He's a nice guy.

311
00:21:59,018 --> 00:22:01,112
You relax.

312
00:22:32,351 --> 00:22:35,048
You won't find anything
of interest in there.

313
00:22:35,221 --> 00:22:38,191
Mr. Lewis.

314
00:22:39,024 --> 00:22:41,184
- Where'd you come from?
- From the rooms where I live.

315
00:22:41,327 --> 00:22:44,263
They're through
that door. I'm sorry.

316
00:22:44,430 --> 00:22:46,365
I'm Paul Dodge.

317
00:22:46,799 --> 00:22:49,098
- I can explain this.
- There's no need.

318
00:22:49,268 --> 00:22:52,136
Sit down. Please.

319
00:22:59,044 --> 00:23:00,307
I've seen this before.

320
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:02,712
- You have?
- Of course.

321
00:23:03,515 --> 00:23:05,416
Your life revolves around pills.

322
00:23:05,584 --> 00:23:08,713
Pills to put you to sleep,
pills to wake you up,

323
00:23:08,888 --> 00:23:09,912
pills to make you happy,

324
00:23:10,756 --> 00:23:13,191
fight pain, suppress
your appetite.

325
00:23:13,792 --> 00:23:15,954
That is what you were
looking for in my desk, isn't it?

326
00:23:16,128 --> 00:23:19,064
Oh, boy, you're
good. You're good.

327
00:23:19,231 --> 00:23:21,791
Well, I've been doing
this for a long time.

328
00:23:22,401 --> 00:23:24,495
Perhaps we ought to
postpone this interview

329
00:23:24,670 --> 00:23:27,799
until after you've had a
chance to settle in for a bit.

330
00:23:27,973 --> 00:23:32,001
Hopefully, by then you
may be a trifle less agitated.

331
00:23:34,713 --> 00:23:36,409
Why don't I just show
you to your room?

332
00:23:37,283 --> 00:23:38,342
Fine. That'd be great.

333
00:23:38,517 --> 00:23:42,181
This telephone will connect
you only to the front desk.

334
00:23:42,354 --> 00:23:44,721
You can't make calls to
anyone outside the clinic

335
00:23:44,890 --> 00:23:47,587
unless and until you've
earned that privilege.

336
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:48,784
Any questions?

337
00:23:48,961 --> 00:23:51,089
- None that I can think of.
- Good.

338
00:23:51,263 --> 00:23:52,526
Your bag is in the closet.

339
00:23:52,698 --> 00:23:55,998
Someone will come around at 6
and show you how we do meals here.

340
00:23:56,168 --> 00:23:57,534
In the meantime,

341
00:23:57,703 --> 00:23:59,467
I want you to get some rest.

342
00:23:59,638 --> 00:24:02,631
I will. Uh, thanks
for your help.

343
00:24:04,243 --> 00:24:07,941
That's what we're
here for, Cliff, to help.

344
00:25:13,212 --> 00:25:14,492
Morning, Mr. Lewis. You all set?

345
00:25:15,647 --> 00:25:17,013
All set.

346
00:25:17,182 --> 00:25:20,812
Is it standard operating procedure
to lock patients in their room?

347
00:25:21,854 --> 00:25:25,018
Well, apparently, you were in
such a state of agitation yesterday

348
00:25:25,190 --> 00:25:28,024
that Dr. Dodge felt it best
to restrict your movements.

349
00:25:28,193 --> 00:25:30,355
Hey, don't worry. It
happens a lot to new patients.

350
00:25:30,529 --> 00:25:32,361
I'm sure after he
finishes your evaluation

351
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:34,898
he'll grant you more privileges.

352
00:25:46,845 --> 00:25:48,313
Please.

353
00:25:55,421 --> 00:25:56,445
Sit.

354
00:25:58,690 --> 00:26:00,784
How you feeling this morning?

355
00:26:01,393 --> 00:26:03,259
Pretty good.

356
00:26:03,429 --> 00:26:05,591
Don't do that.
Don't ever do that.

357
00:26:05,764 --> 00:26:08,598
Don't lie to me. I know
how you're feeling.

358
00:26:08,767 --> 00:26:11,100
By now you must be going
into barbiturate withdrawal.

359
00:26:12,271 --> 00:26:13,603
There's no fooling you is there?

360
00:26:13,772 --> 00:26:17,209
I've watched a lot of young men
and women go through this before.

361
00:26:17,709 --> 00:26:20,372
- Having trouble sleeping?
- I was up most of the night.

362
00:26:20,546 --> 00:26:22,310
Feeling on edge?

363
00:26:22,848 --> 00:26:25,147
- You have no idea.
- Nausea?

364
00:26:25,317 --> 00:26:28,344
- Uh, a little.
- Only a little?

365
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:32,855
My head feels like it's gonna explode.
There's a throbbing behind my eyes.

366
00:26:33,025 --> 00:26:34,493
Maybe we should hospitalize you.

367
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:37,152
No. No, no, no, that
won't be necessary.

368
00:26:37,329 --> 00:26:38,729
We want to minimize
your discomfort.

369
00:26:38,897 --> 00:26:40,195
No. I'll be all right.

370
00:26:40,365 --> 00:26:43,130
- Really. It's okay. Really. Really.
- All right.

371
00:26:43,302 --> 00:26:45,100
All right. We'll try it here.

372
00:26:45,270 --> 00:26:48,672
I'm going to assign
Dr. Hewitt to you.

373
00:26:48,841 --> 00:26:50,434
I'll ask him to give you a call.

374
00:26:51,076 --> 00:26:53,910
You could work out a schedule
for individual and group sessions.

375
00:26:56,014 --> 00:26:58,609
I'm sure you'll make
great progress with him.

376
00:26:59,318 --> 00:27:01,048
Sorry, I'm a little nervous.

377
00:27:01,220 --> 00:27:02,620
I can tell.

378
00:27:02,788 --> 00:27:04,689
Oh, um, about my privileges?

379
00:27:04,857 --> 00:27:06,325
For the moment,
courtyard walks only

380
00:27:06,492 --> 00:27:09,052
and one telephone call a
day. We'll wait till you get better.

381
00:27:09,228 --> 00:27:12,392
That's... Well, I'm a little uncomfortable
with being locked in my room.

382
00:27:12,564 --> 00:27:14,692
It's for your own
good, Cliff. Trust me.

383
00:27:39,625 --> 00:27:41,423
Hey, you can't go out that way.

384
00:27:42,060 --> 00:27:44,291
Well, way to go. You've
just set off the alarm.

385
00:27:47,032 --> 00:27:49,297
Look, it says emergency exit.

386
00:27:49,468 --> 00:27:52,233
I was just trying to
find the, uh, courtyard.

387
00:27:52,404 --> 00:27:54,373
The door to the courtyard's
right across the hall

388
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:56,099
from the main entrance.

389
00:27:56,275 --> 00:27:57,868
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

390
00:27:58,043 --> 00:28:00,069
Hey, well, no harm done.

391
00:28:00,245 --> 00:28:02,045
The security guard's
not gonna like getting up

392
00:28:02,181 --> 00:28:05,743
off his keister for nothing, but
hey, he can use the exercise.

393
00:28:08,687 --> 00:28:11,987
Hey, I'm sorry about the false alarm,
I'm the one who opened the door.

394
00:28:12,157 --> 00:28:13,386
What door?

395
00:28:13,559 --> 00:28:15,425
The emergency
exit in the west wing.

396
00:28:15,594 --> 00:28:17,688
I was looking for the
courtyard. I'm new here.

397
00:28:17,863 --> 00:28:19,525
Well, forget about it.

398
00:28:20,566 --> 00:28:23,229
I've never seen a security
system like this before.

399
00:28:23,402 --> 00:28:24,927
It's a mess.

400
00:28:39,251 --> 00:28:40,651
What are you doing?

401
00:28:40,819 --> 00:28:43,220
I'm moving this picture.

402
00:28:44,389 --> 00:28:48,554
Come on, Dad. Let's go back
on the couch and lie down.

403
00:28:48,727 --> 00:28:51,822
I don't wanna lie down anymore.

404
00:28:52,297 --> 00:28:55,028
Here we go.

405
00:28:55,667 --> 00:29:00,696
Now, be nice and
comfortable. There.

406
00:29:00,872 --> 00:29:03,307
- Here, read a book.
- I don't want to.

407
00:29:06,245 --> 00:29:07,804
Dad, you have a hiatal hernia.

408
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:10,245
The doctor told you to
lie around all weekend.

409
00:29:10,415 --> 00:29:14,079
You can't tell me that moving
one little picture's bad for me.

410
00:29:14,253 --> 00:29:16,620
I'm telling you to do
what the doctor said.

411
00:29:16,788 --> 00:29:19,724
Now, I will have dinner
ready in a couple of minutes.

412
00:29:24,963 --> 00:29:28,161
Just wanted to move
one little picture.

413
00:29:36,742 --> 00:29:37,766
Hello?

414
00:29:37,943 --> 00:29:40,777
Ben. It's Cliff. You're
home. Are you okay?

415
00:29:42,014 --> 00:29:45,143
Yeah. You still at the clinic?

416
00:29:45,317 --> 00:29:48,185
Yes, and I'm only allowed
one phone call a day,

417
00:29:48,353 --> 00:29:50,720
so whatever you do, don't
hang up till I'm through, okay?

418
00:29:50,889 --> 00:29:52,289
You got something?

419
00:29:56,928 --> 00:29:58,920
Oh.

420
00:29:59,097 --> 00:30:00,929
That's interesting.

421
00:30:01,566 --> 00:30:04,798
That's very, very interesting.

422
00:30:04,970 --> 00:30:06,632
Good work.

423
00:30:07,205 --> 00:30:10,369
No, no. You can't
leave the clinic yet.

424
00:30:10,542 --> 00:30:13,478
Maybe in a couple of days.

425
00:30:13,645 --> 00:30:16,945
Cliff, Cliff, Cliff.

426
00:30:17,115 --> 00:30:20,745
Even if Dr. Dodge
does suspect you,

427
00:30:20,919 --> 00:30:22,649
he wouldn't do that.

428
00:30:22,821 --> 00:30:25,814
They don't do
lobotomies anymore.

429
00:30:26,291 --> 00:30:29,352
Just stay, okay? And keep calm.

430
00:30:30,162 --> 00:30:33,064
Good boy. Bye.

431
00:30:49,014 --> 00:30:50,448
Okay?

432
00:32:09,361 --> 00:32:10,886
Leanne!

433
00:32:11,696 --> 00:32:13,722
What? Are you okay?

434
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:16,866
- I got it.
- Got what?

435
00:32:17,035 --> 00:32:19,971
I know how Dr. Dodge did it.

436
00:32:20,138 --> 00:32:22,733
Dad, let me just get
dinner on the table,

437
00:32:22,908 --> 00:32:26,640
and then you can tell
me all about it, okay?

438
00:32:27,379 --> 00:32:30,144
But I know how he did it.

439
00:32:30,682 --> 00:32:32,947
Daggone.

440
00:33:06,351 --> 00:33:10,914
Oh. Dr. Dodge, hello
again. Am I, uh, interrupting?

441
00:33:11,089 --> 00:33:15,390
Not at all. I have, uh, ten whole
minutes before my next appointment.

442
00:33:15,560 --> 00:33:17,085
- Please sit down.
- Thanks.

443
00:33:18,230 --> 00:33:19,789
You look like you
just got out of bed.

444
00:33:20,932 --> 00:33:24,096
I did. I haven't
been feeling well.

445
00:33:24,269 --> 00:33:25,567
Oh.

446
00:33:25,737 --> 00:33:27,467
- Anything serious?
- No.

447
00:33:27,639 --> 00:33:31,508
They ran some tests. The
doctor thinks I have a hiatal hernia.

448
00:33:31,676 --> 00:33:34,544
It's somewhere here
in the digestive tract.

449
00:33:34,713 --> 00:33:38,445
Me, I think it's this case. I've been
so obsessed with trying to figure out

450
00:33:38,617 --> 00:33:42,679
who actually killed Allan
Rafferty, it made me sick.

451
00:33:42,854 --> 00:33:46,723
Or maybe it's, uh, that deep
down in your heart of hearts,

452
00:33:46,892 --> 00:33:48,292
you know your client's guilty.

453
00:33:48,460 --> 00:33:50,554
No, no, no.

454
00:33:50,729 --> 00:33:53,528
I'm more convinced than
ever that he's innocent.

455
00:33:58,270 --> 00:34:00,330
I know about the door.

456
00:34:01,706 --> 00:34:04,767
- The door?
- The emergency exit door

457
00:34:04,943 --> 00:34:06,844
at the end of the east wing

458
00:34:07,012 --> 00:34:10,107
which triggers the alarm
when nobody's even near it.

459
00:34:10,782 --> 00:34:12,774
Bad connections or something.

460
00:34:13,618 --> 00:34:16,315
The security guard told me
that just before the murder

461
00:34:16,488 --> 00:34:20,687
it false alarmed three
times in four days.

462
00:34:21,526 --> 00:34:24,758
We're just about to put in
an entire new security system.

463
00:34:24,930 --> 00:34:26,730
We're putting up with
the bugs in the old one,

464
00:34:26,898 --> 00:34:29,129
rather than get them
repaired, I'm afraid.

465
00:34:29,301 --> 00:34:30,325
Oh.

466
00:34:30,502 --> 00:34:32,596
So you knew about the problem?

467
00:34:32,771 --> 00:34:34,899
Yes. Of course.

468
00:34:37,042 --> 00:34:42,504
Well, that means that when you
opened the emergency exit door

469
00:34:42,681 --> 00:34:44,775
at the end of the west wing,

470
00:34:44,950 --> 00:34:48,409
you knew the guard would
automatically go to the door

471
00:34:48,587 --> 00:34:52,649
at the end of the east wing, which
had triggered the alarm, didn't you?

472
00:34:58,496 --> 00:35:03,400
Have you reached the conclusion
that I killed Allan Rafferty?

473
00:35:03,568 --> 00:35:05,696
I did that a long time ago.

474
00:35:05,870 --> 00:35:08,840
I just wasn't sure how
you did it. Now I am.

475
00:35:12,077 --> 00:35:18,074
As with all obsessions, Mr. Matlock,
this one is ill-advised and ill-fated.

476
00:35:18,249 --> 00:35:21,447
You're wrong. I can prove it.

477
00:35:26,791 --> 00:35:28,225
Zoe?

478
00:35:29,027 --> 00:35:31,428
Hi, Zoe. This'll
only take a minute.

479
00:35:31,596 --> 00:35:33,827
Zoe Altman, I want you
to meet Ben Matlock.

480
00:35:33,999 --> 00:35:35,627
- Mr. Matlock, nice to meet you.
- Hello.

481
00:35:35,800 --> 00:35:37,632
Miss Altman's a patient here.

482
00:35:37,802 --> 00:35:40,795
Mr. Matlock represents the man
accused of killing Dr. Rafferty.

483
00:35:40,972 --> 00:35:45,706
Now, I'd like you to tell him where
you were the evening Dr. Rafferty died.

484
00:35:45,877 --> 00:35:49,507
Well, I was with you. I had missed
a session, I was making it up.

485
00:35:50,515 --> 00:35:51,574
At what time?

486
00:35:51,750 --> 00:35:54,743
About 9:00 until almost 11.

487
00:35:57,155 --> 00:36:00,023
Do you usually see
patients so late at night?

488
00:36:00,592 --> 00:36:01,616
Of course not.

489
00:36:02,293 --> 00:36:05,752
Since Miss Altman missed her
session in order to attend a funeral,

490
00:36:05,930 --> 00:36:08,923
I felt that seeing her promptly
was of the utmost importance.

491
00:36:10,335 --> 00:36:13,066
But you told the police
you were alone all evening.

492
00:36:13,238 --> 00:36:16,731
Well, frankly, the media's been
having such a field day with articles

493
00:36:16,908 --> 00:36:19,878
about therapists having
affairs with their patients, that I...

494
00:36:20,045 --> 00:36:23,106
Well, I thought it better not even to
suggest a compromising situation.

495
00:36:26,284 --> 00:36:29,311
I see. Of course. Well, I...

496
00:36:37,128 --> 00:36:38,391
Dad.

497
00:36:39,497 --> 00:36:40,726
There you are.

498
00:36:40,899 --> 00:36:42,959
You promised me you
would lie around all weekend.

499
00:36:43,134 --> 00:36:46,571
What do I have to do,
watch you for 24 hours?

500
00:36:47,272 --> 00:36:49,969
I thought I had
him. I was sure of it.

501
00:36:50,842 --> 00:36:52,208
Dr. Dodge.

502
00:36:53,044 --> 00:36:56,776
He's got an alibi.
He didn't do it.

503
00:36:56,948 --> 00:37:00,476
I'm sorry, Dad. Now, come on.

504
00:37:27,846 --> 00:37:29,838
I hope you don't plan
to go swimming in that.

505
00:37:30,014 --> 00:37:32,506
No, just looking around.

506
00:37:32,684 --> 00:37:34,744
Like to look at half
naked men, huh?

507
00:37:37,322 --> 00:37:38,688
Kind of.

508
00:37:40,925 --> 00:37:44,327
Actually, I'm just taking one
more look at the scene of the crime.

509
00:37:44,496 --> 00:37:46,727
Always seems to help my father.

510
00:37:47,499 --> 00:37:49,161
But I'm not my father.

511
00:37:49,334 --> 00:37:50,461
What are you, a cop?

512
00:37:50,635 --> 00:37:54,800
An attorney. Leanne Mclntyre,
co-counsel for Terry Landis.

513
00:37:56,274 --> 00:37:58,641
- You a patient here?
- Assistant chef.

514
00:37:58,810 --> 00:38:01,109
But they let me use the
pool when I'm not working.

515
00:38:01,279 --> 00:38:03,646
- Sam Badetcher.
- Nice to meet you.

516
00:38:05,316 --> 00:38:07,581
- You swim laps every day?
- Half a mile, every day.

517
00:38:07,752 --> 00:38:08,776
Except Sunday.

518
00:38:12,190 --> 00:38:14,091
Holy cow.

519
00:38:17,829 --> 00:38:21,061
- Would you read that for me, please?
- Sure.

520
00:38:22,267 --> 00:38:25,101
- Seventy-five degrees.
- Thank you.

521
00:38:25,270 --> 00:38:27,136
The other one had
nice big numbers on it

522
00:38:27,305 --> 00:38:29,501
that I could read all
the way across the pool.

523
00:38:29,674 --> 00:38:32,644
Why they replaced it with that
piece of junk, I'll never know.

524
00:38:40,552 --> 00:38:42,214
Anyone say why they replaced it?

525
00:38:42,387 --> 00:38:45,448
No. Somebody decided
they wanted a change.

526
00:38:45,623 --> 00:38:47,148
Dr. Dodge, probably.

527
00:38:58,002 --> 00:39:00,028
- Dad.
- Yeah?

528
00:39:02,307 --> 00:39:04,105
What are you doing?

529
00:39:06,444 --> 00:39:07,935
I don't feel good.

530
00:39:08,112 --> 00:39:09,637
Oh.

531
00:39:09,814 --> 00:39:12,045
I bet I know what'll
make you feel better.

532
00:39:12,217 --> 00:39:15,415
- What?
- Seven magic words.

533
00:39:15,587 --> 00:39:17,818
I was wrong, and you were right.

534
00:39:19,157 --> 00:39:21,717
- About what?
- Dr. Dodge.

535
00:39:22,527 --> 00:39:23,790
He did it, Dad.

536
00:39:32,270 --> 00:39:34,671
Tell me something, Dr. Dodge.

537
00:39:34,839 --> 00:39:40,107
How did the <i>National Informer</i>
find out that Terry Landis

538
00:39:40,278 --> 00:39:42,008
was a patient at your clinic?

539
00:39:43,014 --> 00:39:46,542
I have no idea. Maybe you
should ask the <i>National Informer.</i>

540
00:39:46,718 --> 00:39:50,018
They're shielded from,
uh, revealing their sources.

541
00:39:50,188 --> 00:39:53,716
So how about it? Who
told the <i>National Informer</i>

542
00:39:53,892 --> 00:39:56,088
of Mr. Landis'
treatment at your clinic?

543
00:39:56,261 --> 00:39:57,752
I wouldn't know, counselor.

544
00:39:57,929 --> 00:40:01,127
Well, you have access
to all his therapists' notes.

545
00:40:01,299 --> 00:40:04,235
I mean, his visits
with Terry and all that?

546
00:40:04,402 --> 00:40:07,804
Yes. As head of the
clinic, I consult on all cases.

547
00:40:07,972 --> 00:40:13,343
Yeah. There are details in
here of his early childhood,

548
00:40:13,511 --> 00:40:17,539
stepfather abuse,
early alcohol abuse.

549
00:40:18,249 --> 00:40:21,310
That information could only
have come from one source,

550
00:40:23,388 --> 00:40:26,847
- his therapist, don't you agree?
- I told you, sir. I don't know.

551
00:40:28,927 --> 00:40:31,487
- You didn't leak the story, did you?
- Absolutely not.

552
00:40:31,663 --> 00:40:35,828
It would ruin you and
your clinic if word got out

553
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:40,870
that your staff was leaking patient
information to the <i>National Informer.</i>

554
00:40:41,039 --> 00:40:44,874
- Well...
- Then when the story did come out,

555
00:40:45,043 --> 00:40:48,707
you knew only one
person could have leaked it,

556
00:40:48,880 --> 00:40:51,611
outside of Terry Landis himself,

557
00:40:51,783 --> 00:40:55,948
and that was his
therapist, Allan Rafferty.

558
00:40:56,120 --> 00:41:00,956
So rather than let his greed
destroy your clinic, you killed him.

559
00:41:01,125 --> 00:41:04,289
Objection. Seems Mr. Matlock
is confused as to exactly

560
00:41:04,462 --> 00:41:06,556
- who's on trial here.
- Uh, Your Honor,

561
00:41:06,731 --> 00:41:10,293
the evidence against my
client is purely circumstantial.

562
00:41:10,468 --> 00:41:12,198
I must be allowed to demonstrate

563
00:41:12,370 --> 00:41:15,829
that those very same circumstances
might make someone else,

564
00:41:16,007 --> 00:41:18,841
maybe even this gentleman
here, appear just as guilty.

565
00:41:19,477 --> 00:41:21,946
- Overruled.
- Thanks.

566
00:41:22,480 --> 00:41:27,680
Uh, Carter Jacobson, night
watchman there at your clinic,

567
00:41:27,852 --> 00:41:30,686
ready to testify that
he discovered the body

568
00:41:30,855 --> 00:41:32,949
at, uh, 11:00 that night.

569
00:41:33,124 --> 00:41:36,788
Now, where were you
between, say 10 and 11?

570
00:41:36,961 --> 00:41:40,261
I was in my office, conducting
a private therapy session.

571
00:41:40,431 --> 00:41:44,527
Individual therapy session with a
patient, uh, Miss Altman, right over there.

572
00:41:45,636 --> 00:41:48,504
Miss Altman is ready to testify

573
00:41:49,974 --> 00:41:54,378
that you left your office
around 10:30, right?

574
00:41:55,380 --> 00:41:57,975
I suddenly remembered
that, uh, I promised someone

575
00:41:58,149 --> 00:42:00,243
I would leave a package
for them at the front desk.

576
00:42:00,418 --> 00:42:05,322
She's also ready to testify
that you were gone a good five,

577
00:42:05,490 --> 00:42:08,221
maybe ten minutes.
What took you so long?

578
00:42:08,393 --> 00:42:11,591
I stopped off in my apartment
to make a telephone call.

579
00:42:11,763 --> 00:42:14,358
There's no record of your
making a phone call that night.

580
00:42:14,532 --> 00:42:16,194
The line was busy,
I never got through.

581
00:42:16,367 --> 00:42:18,996
I see. I see.

582
00:42:19,170 --> 00:42:22,698
So all we have is your word
that you were in your room

583
00:42:22,874 --> 00:42:25,810
trying to place a call at
the time of the murder,

584
00:42:25,977 --> 00:42:30,039
the same as all we have is Terry's
word that he was in his room sleeping.

585
00:42:30,214 --> 00:42:33,912
The police found his necklace
in the pool that evening, not mine.

586
00:42:34,085 --> 00:42:36,486
Don't you have a pass
key to all the rooms?

587
00:42:36,654 --> 00:42:37,815
Yes. But...

588
00:42:37,989 --> 00:42:41,323
So you could've
slipped into Terry's room,

589
00:42:41,492 --> 00:42:44,621
stolen his necklace
while he was asleep,

590
00:42:44,796 --> 00:42:48,460
dropped it in the pool
after you killed Dr. Rafferty.

591
00:42:48,633 --> 00:42:50,499
I did not kill Allan Rafferty.

592
00:42:50,668 --> 00:42:54,127
Are you sure you didn't
give the guard the envelope,

593
00:42:54,305 --> 00:42:57,241
then walk up the
hall of the west wing,

594
00:42:57,408 --> 00:42:59,741
open the emergency exit there,

595
00:42:59,911 --> 00:43:04,474
knowing the guard would go
to the door at the east wing,

596
00:43:04,649 --> 00:43:06,174
because it had
triggered the alarm,

597
00:43:06,350 --> 00:43:09,081
it had done it three
times the week before

598
00:43:09,253 --> 00:43:12,883
and you knew the police
would later suspect,

599
00:43:13,057 --> 00:43:16,653
because of his necklace,
that Terry had done it.

600
00:43:16,828 --> 00:43:21,789
You knew that nobody would
suspect that you were actually the one

601
00:43:21,966 --> 00:43:25,562
who went in that pool and
drowned that wayward employee.

602
00:43:26,871 --> 00:43:29,067
Mr. Matlock, I am
not an attorney,

603
00:43:29,240 --> 00:43:32,074
but it seems to me you are
coming very close to slander, sir.

604
00:43:32,243 --> 00:43:39,275
Close? Yeah, I'd be knee deep
in it if it wasn't for one little thing.

605
00:43:40,151 --> 00:43:41,744
I've got proof.

606
00:43:48,493 --> 00:43:53,625
Mr. Sam Badetcher,
assistant chef at your clinic,

607
00:43:54,398 --> 00:43:56,060
is ready to testify

608
00:43:56,968 --> 00:43:59,961
that there used to
be a thermometer

609
00:44:00,138 --> 00:44:04,542
that hung on the north side
of the pool at the deep end.

610
00:44:04,709 --> 00:44:10,307
It was there when he swam laps
the afternoon before the murder.

611
00:44:11,883 --> 00:44:16,617
But there's no sign of it here,
see, in this picture the police took

612
00:44:16,787 --> 00:44:18,779
of the body some
seven hours later.

613
00:44:18,956 --> 00:44:23,951
He's also ready to testify that
when he swam laps the next day,

614
00:44:24,362 --> 00:44:26,729
there was a new
thermometer in its place.

615
00:44:27,832 --> 00:44:29,630
What happened to
the old one, Dr. Dodge?

616
00:44:31,369 --> 00:44:34,999
I don't know. It, uh,
suddenly disappeared.

617
00:44:36,274 --> 00:44:37,708
Yeah.

618
00:44:38,142 --> 00:44:41,635
My daughter's not only a fine
lawyer, she's a fine detective.

619
00:44:42,446 --> 00:44:47,282
That thermometer disappeared
because it got broken in the struggle

620
00:44:47,451 --> 00:44:50,444
while you were
drowning Allan Rafferty.

621
00:44:50,621 --> 00:44:52,055
It either got
broken accidentally,

622
00:44:52,223 --> 00:44:55,125
or he broke it on purpose to
use as a weapon against you.

623
00:44:55,293 --> 00:44:57,853
But anyway, when it was over
and your colleague was dead,

624
00:44:58,029 --> 00:44:59,520
you couldn't leave it there.

625
00:44:59,697 --> 00:45:01,063
Might incriminate you.

626
00:45:01,232 --> 00:45:04,464
So you scooped up that broken
thermometer, took it with you.

627
00:45:05,403 --> 00:45:09,465
But Miss Altman was waiting in
your office. Time was of the essence.

628
00:45:09,640 --> 00:45:11,666
You could always
get rid of it later.

629
00:45:12,276 --> 00:45:15,178
But you had to do something
with it right then and there.

630
00:45:16,280 --> 00:45:18,249
And so you hid it.

631
00:45:20,585 --> 00:45:22,349
Under the mattress in your bed.

632
00:45:24,322 --> 00:45:25,756
This bed.

633
00:45:26,691 --> 00:45:31,095
The one the police and
my daughter, with a warrant,

634
00:45:31,262 --> 00:45:33,663
took out of your place this
morning. What do you say, doctor?

635
00:45:35,499 --> 00:45:37,058
No.

636
00:45:37,235 --> 00:45:43,869
Then why did the police
lab find mercury in the fibers

637
00:45:44,041 --> 00:45:45,873
at the bottom of your mattress,

638
00:45:46,043 --> 00:45:49,343
and in fibers at the top
of the box spring under it?

639
00:45:49,547 --> 00:45:53,917
Because you got rid of that
thermometer as quick as you could

640
00:45:54,085 --> 00:45:56,020
after the police left.

641
00:45:56,187 --> 00:46:01,023
But the broken stem had already
leaked mercury into your bed.

642
00:46:01,192 --> 00:46:03,093
And it's still there.

643
00:46:03,894 --> 00:46:07,296
You killed Allan Rafferty.

644
00:46:12,436 --> 00:46:13,870
I told you.

645
00:46:21,045 --> 00:46:22,536
It is great to be free.

646
00:46:22,713 --> 00:46:25,342
And it's a kick to know that
our system of justice works.

647
00:46:25,516 --> 00:46:27,644
And not just on TV,
but in real life too.

648
00:46:27,818 --> 00:46:29,582
- What are you gonna do now?
- What next?

649
00:46:29,754 --> 00:46:32,223
My agent here says the
networks are gonna make my story

650
00:46:32,390 --> 00:46:35,292
into a mini-series,
starring me as myself.

651
00:46:35,459 --> 00:46:37,189
Thank you.

652
00:46:42,533 --> 00:46:44,399
What a jerk, the guy
didn't even say thank you.

653
00:46:44,568 --> 00:46:47,367
Yeah he did. Got
his check right here.

654
00:46:48,406 --> 00:46:49,926
- Let's get something to eat.
- Hungry?

655
00:46:50,074 --> 00:46:51,599
- I'm starving.
- Me too.

656
00:46:51,776 --> 00:46:53,904
Three hot dogs, all the way.

657
00:46:56,013 --> 00:46:57,379
He lives.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
